```markdown
# How a run ends, and what a failure leaves behind

Every run ends with exactly one turn-ending comment on the issue or pull request it
worked on. It is how the thread learns the turn is over, it is what takes the
`atomaton/in-progress` guard's place in the timeline, and the footer — the run link,
the token count, the metrics link — is written onto it.

## The two ways the comment is written

**The agent writes it.** The last thing the model says becomes the comment...

**A session-ending tool writes it.** `create_pr`, `launch_sub_agent`,
`request_close_issue` and `reload_environment` end the session as part of their
work; the footer is then appended to the comment that ended it, so the run still
has exactly one ending.

## A failure is also an ending

When the agent itself fails, the runner converts the failure into the comment:
the failure notice is the turn-ending comment — it says the session was saved,
how to continue (`/agent`) and how to start clean (`/agent recover`) — and the
node is back with a person, so the next comment on the thread resumes normally.

The colour follows the comment. **A failure that was reported leaves the run
green.** A green run with a failure notice is not a lie: the notice is the run's
report, and a red run beside it would only repeat that news in Notifications,
where it drowns the runs that have nothing else to say it with. **A failure that
could not be reported leaves the run red** — the notice could not be written, or
a machinery step died: the checkout, the session save, the result comment. Red is
then the only signal the run leaves, and the workflow log says what the thread
does not.

## When there is no comment at all

A run that dies before its reporter can run — or whose comment `gh` refuses —
leaves no ending on the thread. The red run is the whole signal, and the restore
is the same for every kind of death:

**Re-run the failed run.** It resumes from the saved session and writes the
ending it owed. (`gh workflow run atomaton-runner.yml -f number=N -f agent=X` does
the same by hand.)

A related shape, from the other side: a comment that keeps vanishing with no run
arriving means a run died without writing its ending — the thread's last event is
still "the ball is with an agent", so the guard keeps removing what follows it.
Re-run the failed run; once it posts its ending, comments go through again.
```

